  "text": "Subscribe to skip adsAdvertisementEnvironmentThis New “Devil Flower” Species Doesn’t PhotosynthesizeIt’s a rare plant with an “enigmatic and devil-like essence”DNBy David Nield1:00 PM CDT on September 8, 2026Add Nautilus to GoogleDiscovered in the forests of the Thong Pha Phum National Park in Thailand, the leafless “devil flower” looks like a small, charred gremlin staring at you with fiery eyes. In fact, as soon as you see the newly identified plant species Thismia daemona, you understand the nickname. Subscribe to skip adsFeatured VideoPowered By 10 Just How Many Black Holes Are There in the Universe?ShareNextStayThe tiny horns protruding from the top and the glaring eyes are actually individual fleshy outer parts of the plant known as tepals, and their color and the lack of leaves gives something else away: There’s no chlorophyll in there, and the plant doesn’t use photosynthesis for nourishment. Instead, T. daemona gets its sustenance from drawing in nutrients through fungi in the soil.The “horns” and “eyes” are actually parts of the flower called tepals. Credit: Sahut ChantanaorrapintSubscribe to skip adsAdvertisement",
